<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">4543456</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2139467</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>INHIBITORY AND STIMULATORY EFFECTS OF CONCANAVALIN A ON THE RESPONSE OF MOUSE SPLEEN CELL SUSPENSIONS TO ANTIGEN </article-title><subtitle>II. EVIDENCE FOR SEPARATE STIMULATORY AND INHIBITORY CELLS</subtitle></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Dutton</surname><given-names>Richard W.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x1de66b0N0x3278a38">From the Department of Biology, University of California, San Diego, La Jolla, California 92037</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>30</day><month>11</month><year>1973</year></pub-date><volume>138</volume><issue>6</issue><fpage>1496</fpage><lpage>1505</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>9</day><month>8</month><year>1973</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1973 by The Rockefeller University Press</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> The addition of concanavalin A to mouse spleen cell suspensions can either inhibit or stimulate the immune response to heterologous erythrocytes according to the experimental conditions. Evidence is presented which is incompatible with the hypothesis that these two effects are mediated by a single cell and which favors the hypothesis of separate inhibitor and stimulatory cells. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>